    "urn": "urn:li:activity:7433019629066715136",
    "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7433019629066715136/",
    "text": "Jack Dorsey's company, Block, just fired 40% of its workforce and the share price spiked 23%. I'm sorry to tell you, but this is not a once-off. This is the start of something big. \n\n2026 will be the beginning of the mass layoffs because of AI. As he says, most companies are going to do the same. And no, these jobs are not coming back. \n\nI always try to be really positive in my writing about AI because it seems to really upset people when I talk about the possibility of jobs being lost, but I think we've turned that corner unfortunately. \n\nYes, in the past we had job layoffs that may have mentioned AI, but they were an excuse for over-bloated companies to cut heads. But with the advancements we've seen in the tech in the last couple of months alone, boards are figuring out what the future is going to look like. \n\nElon started it all slashing 80% of Twitter and still keeping the lights on. Many other companies are realising they can do this too. And they're foaming at the mouth of being able to cut costs and improve margins by utilising the AI tools that are going to start emerging in the next few months. \n\nHow many people's jobs are just pushing numbers around, collating data, preparing reports and PowerPoint decks? I think we've seen over the last while that a lot of this is going to be able to be done in minutes, not days. \n\nI've been playing around with some tools recently. I've built interactive dashboards in a couple of minutes from one single data source. And I've turned a boring report into an interactive dashboard, animated presentation, in literally a couple of prompts. \n\nWhat we've seen from OpenClaw being able to actually go off and do meaningful work on its own, sometimes producing results you didn't even ask for, these tools will be released by most of the major LLM providers. OpenAI is even talking about a $20,000 a month plan, which will be better than a PhD research student. \n\nBut Colin, all technology produces new jobs and so the market grows. It's always done that. I'm sorry to tell you, but technology has always produced tools that need humans to operate them. AI does not. It's the only thing that can genuinely do meaningful work on its own. And what jobs do you think AI is going to create that it cannot do itself? \n\nI've been very worried about this for many years and secretly hoping I would be wrong, but I think we're about to see the complete apocalypse happen very, very soon. The markets will be disrupted. Getting a job will be near impossible. I'm really not looking forward to it. \n\nCan someone please tell me I'm wrong and that everything is gonna be okay?",
