    "urn": "urn:li:activity:7412445310989275137",
    "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7412445310989275137/",
    "text": "Here are my predictions for 2026: the year AI stops being impressive demos and becomes real infrastructure.\n\nVibe coding goes fully mainstream. Engineers write far less code and move up the stack to architecture and problem framing, while AI handles most code.\n\nAI coding reaches the point where we manage teams of AI agents doing the work, with humans supervising outcomes rather than writing most of the code. AI agents show up as digital co-workers, and personal assistants finally manage calendars, email, and to-do lists properly.\n\nI thought 2025 would be the year of AI agents, but it wasn’t. The models weren’t reliable enough. That has now changed. With better autonomy, reasoning, and tool use, 2026 is when agents become dependable and genuinely useful.\n\nIn hindsight, 2025 was a transition year. Model capability, benchmark performance, and release velocity exceeded expectations, but agents and real tools lagged. That gap closes in 2026.\n\nWe move from chatbots to autonomous action. You give an AI agent a task, it plans the steps, executes them, and manages the workflow end to end. Multi-agent systems become easier to launch and far more practical.\n\nThe human-computer interface shifts. Voice becomes good enough that speaking to computers feels natural, fast, and reliable.\n\nSmart glasses and wearables take off. Phones move to the pocket while AI lives in your glasses and headphones, edging closer to a “Samantha from Her” experience. Smart glasses start getting genuinely capable displays.\n\nTesla cracks FSD, and that becomes a defining moment. The share price responds as confidence shifts from promises to proof. Robo-taxis go live across the US and possibly parts of Europe, creating a true autonomous mobility-on-demand moment. \n\nOptimus moves beyond demos into genuinely useful autonomous work, with clearer signals on production and scale. Figure and several Chinese robotics companies also deliver real-world breakthroughs.\n\nChina’s rise accelerates more broadly. Chinese AI models continue improving rapidly, with more DeepSeek-style moments, alongside major progress in EVs and robotics.\n\nAI becomes more central at home. Conversational, ChatGPT-like intelligence embedded into Alexa and Google Home makes AI feel normal for families and kids. Apple has dropped the ball, but Siri needs to truly arrive next year.\n\nSmaller, faster, more specialised models proliferate. On-device AI becomes standard for privacy and speed. Open source closes much of the gap with state-of-the-art models, cheaply.\n\nModel efficiency improves dramatically. Progress comes from better architectures, not just bigger data centres. World models reduce hallucinations and improve trust.\n\nAI's exponential curve continues. Hiring slows, layoffs increase, pushback grows, and serious conversations about AGI and universal basic income become unavoidable.\n\n2026 is when it all comes together: usable AI infrastructure, orchestrated agents, reliable autonomy, and real-world integration.",
    "date": "1 month ago",
    "likes": 43,
    "comments": 18,
    "impressions": 2017,
