    "urn": "urn:li:activity:7062063882449620994",
    "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7062063882449620994/",
    "text": "Avatars are coming to Microsoft Teams. If you are sick, hungover or just out of bed, activate your Avatar and it will move with your voice in the meeting!\n\nLet's face it, we have been becoming more and more digital for years now - we spend more time communicating with friends and family digitally than in real life, and this is only going to continue.\n\nMost kids who game are very used to representing themselves digitally as an avatar. Many spend more time socialising in-game than in real life - and they take their digital appearance very seriously. \n\nIt's very common for them to prefer to spend money accessorising their avatar in-game over buying clothes in real life. Kids have a very acute value for digital assets that many older generations just don't get - but it's coming!\n\nYou can now use an iPhone to scan your face and make a realistic avatar - could this be the primary way you show yourself online in the future?\n\nNO WAY - I WON'T DO THAT! I want to look at them - not a picture of them!! I want to see the real person!\n\nWell, people said this simiar video calling many years ago too...\n\nWhat if this avatar was properly photo realistic and moved exactly like they do? Are the lines getting blurred? \n\nIn a few years when we have AR/MR glasses, maybe we'll have an avatar projected in front of us so you can have a virtual in person meeting with colleagues from around the world.\n\nSci-fi nonsense that will never work? \nOr is it the future?\n\n\nhashtag\n#avatars\n\nhashtag\n#mixedreality\n\nhashtag\n#microsoftteams",
    "date": "2 years ago",
    "likes": 22,
    "comments": 9,
    "impressions": 0,
