    "urn": "urn:li:activity:7022960443354718209",
    "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7022960443354718209/",
    "text": "I've really enjoyed watching the Boston Dynamics robots evolve over the last 10 years. They've gone from clunky, noisy primitive machines, to fluid, dymanic, agile dancing characters that captured the hearts and minds of the internet.\n\nThis latest version of Atlas has added dexterity with hands, or hooks that will be interchangeable with anything we can imagine. It can now easily help out on a building site so the potential in a factory, office and even the home will soon be game changing.\n\nTesla are also working on their own robot. Despite only starting a year ago they have a very impressive first prototype and will no doubt catch up with Boston Dynamics in a few years, but with the added benefit of their AI and self driving software that will make the difference to the robots usability working around people. Elon Musk said If you think the market for electric vehicles is big, wait until you see the size of the market for manual labour! \n\nThe target price for these units is $20,000. A business will easily pay $20k for an employee who works almost 24/7, for no additonal salary, and never complains or calls in sick. I really do worry about the job loss implications...\n\nMcDonalds recently debuted a fully autonomous restaurant where everything done by robots. While that may not happen in your local restaurant any time soon, they will be replacing humans with the more dangerous frying jobs in many restaurants this year. If successful and it continues in this and other sectors, I'm not sure if our society is equipt to deal with such a rapid sweep of blue collar job losses.\n\nBut it's not just blue collar as we've seen how AI will replace more skilled jobs also. It's roles like financial analysts, legal researchers, customer service & telemarketing will soon be easily done by AI.\n\nThe saving grace however is that new technologies always create a significant amount of new jobs (think of all the internet based jobs that didnt exist 25 years ago). Sam Altman CEO of Open AI said his goal is to use the value that GPT will create for the benefit of humanity. So maybe robots and AI will take over the jobs we don't like to do and elevate us to work on what we are more interested in and passionate about. Or perhaps if we can make basic income work, working may be optional?\n\nIt won't be long until Robots are everywhere and AI is making our lives easier in ways we can barely comprehend right now. Maybe we will have that robot cleaner to do the housework like in the Jetsons!\n\n\"I, for one, welcome our new Robot overloads!\"\n\n\nhashtag\n#ai \nhashtag\n#robotics \nhashtag\n#futurism",
    "date": "3 years ago",
    "likes": 17,
    "comments": 5,
    "impressions": 0,
    "_debug": ""
